{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at exact clean head fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68, succeeding 0d87b56b. Companion to the cmd/gs artifact f68313f3 and the docs artifact at this same head; all three must be co-signed together.\n\nTWO ADDITIONS, BOTH BECAUSE A RUNBOOK QUESTION NEEDED THEM. ArtifactQuery gains two optional fields. state selects live, retired, succeeded or all, and defaults to live, which is byte-identical to the old behaviour — an existing HTTP or MCP response does not change. reaches follows artifact-to-artifact provenance transitively from an anchor path and returns the population that still reaches it, which is the question AGENTS.md's migration gate asks and which no per-event walk can answer. BuildReviewGate is new: it computes the quiet-wave counts — named, awaiting, unresolved — and the approved heads a caller must then ask Git about.\n\nWHY THE CLOSURE LIVES HERE RATHER THAN IN gs provenance, which is the one place this head departs from its request. The request said to extend gs provenance and not to write a closure beside it. But RenderProvenance is already transitive, and it takes one event, so it cannot answer \"which live artifacts still reach this anchor\" however far it is extended. The closure belongs next to BuildArtifactPage because it selects over the artifact population, which is what that builder does. A reviewer who disagrees should block the head on it rather than let it pass unremarked.\n\nTHE SETTLEMENT SEMANTICS ARE THE SUBTLE PART, and they are where the jq this replaces had two bugs worth not reproducing. BuildReviewGate keys settlement by the request event through the commitment, not by artifact name, because several requests can name one artifact and a verdict on one of them must not settle the others. It reads projection.reviews, which the fold populates only from reports it judged effective, rather than filtering statements on kind, because a malformed or unauthorized report carrying an approved verdict must not be able to release a gate. It counts either canonical verdict, since waiting cannot turn a changes-requested into an approval. And it fails closed on a reference it cannot resolve, reporting unresolved as a number rather than silently dropping the row — a gate that can declare quiet while work is outstanding is worse than no gate.\n\nMUTATION-CHECKED, and these are the checks that matter because the counts look plausible either way. Keying settlement by artifact instead of request collapses awaiting from 3 to 1 and fails TestSettlementIsKeyedByRequestNotByArtifact and TestAChangesRequestedVerdictSettlesToo. Stopping the anchor walk after one hop fails TestAnchorFollowsArtifactProvenancePastTheFirstHop with \"far reaches an artifact at `.` and is missing\". Collapsing Git's three ancestry answers into two fails TestApprovedHeadsAreSplitThreeWaysNotTwo, where a commit this clone does not have must not be reported as measured-out-of-branch. Each went red and each was restored.\n\nCHECKED AGAINST THE JQ IT REPLACES rather than against itself: named, awaiting and unresolved read 234, 6 and 220, identical to the jq; the approved-head count is 57 against jq's 58, and the difference is jq's null row — a review carrying no head, which names no commit for Git to answer about. That discrepancy is explained rather than averaged away.\n\nARCHITECTURE: no contract moves. This is projection-layer selection beside the builders that already do it, reached from the CLI through the same code path rather than a second implementation, which is what condition 2 required. The resident, MCP and HTTP surfaces are unchanged in behaviour.\n\nSECURITY: no authority or trust boundary is touched. The additions are read-only selection over a projection the caller can already fetch whole; nothing is signed, admitted or refused differently. The one boundary-adjacent behaviour is that BuildReviewGate fails closed rather than open, which is the correct direction for something whose output can release an irreversible migration.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 27 packages ok; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0, no data race; gofmt empty; diff-check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68","path":"internal/statusview"}}